# OpenSPARC T1: Modern Open-Source Port

This repository contains the OpenSPARC T1 (UltraSPARC T1 / Niagara) design
database originally released by Sun Microsystems under the GNU General Public
License, version 2.

The original release targets a proprietary, Solaris-era flow: VCS,
NC-Verilog, Vera, the SAS and Midas tools, and Synopsys/Synplicity synthesis.
None of those dependencies are available in a current Linux environment. This
port makes the original RTL usable with modern open-source Verilog tools.
Stage 1 makes the RTL compile with Icarus Verilog, lint with Verilator, and
pass a Yosys hierarchy check. Stage 2 adds a self-contained, pure-Verilog core
simulation smoke test that runs on both Icarus and Verilator and does not use
Vera, PLI, SAS, or any Solaris binary. Stage 3 adds an FPGA wrapper and a
nextpnr/Gowin implementation flow, plus an ASIC flow based on LibreLane and
the sky130 PDK.

The T1 RTL is Verilog-2001. It is not VHDL, so GHDL is not used for the design
itself. A mixed-language environment can be built on top of the Verilog RTL,
but that is outside the current scope.

## Verified toolchain

The checks below were run on Linux x86-64 with:

| Tool | Version | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Python | 3.14 | File-list and flow generation |
| Icarus Verilog | 12.0 | `core1` RTL compiles, top `sparc`; smoke test passes |
| Verilator | 5.032 | 1364-2001 lint, 0 errors; smoke test passes |
| Yosys | 0.52 | RTL read and `hierarchy -check -top sparc` |
| Yosys | 0.70+221 | Gowin synthesis of the full core |
| nextpnr-himbaechel | 0.11.1-80 | GW5AST-138C place and route |
| gowin_pack (apycula) | oss-cad-suite | Gowin bitstream packing |
| LibreLane | 3.0.17 | sky130 RTL-to-GDS leaf-block flow |
| sky130 PDK | CIEL 792ef67 | sky130A standard-cell libraries |

These are minimum-tested versions, not hard requirements. Recent releases of
the same tools should work.

## Quick start

Install `make`, Python 3, Icarus Verilog, Verilator, and Yosys. From the
repository root run:

```sh
make -C port all
```

This generates the file lists and runs all three checks. Individual checks are
also available:

```sh
make -C port icarus
make -C port verilator
make -C port yosys

make -C port smoke           # Stage 2 core boot test (Icarus)
make -C port fpga-sim        # Stage 3 FPGA wrapper boot test
make -C port fpga-smoke      # fast nextpnr/Gowin toolchain bitstream
make -C port fpga            # full-core FPGA bitstream (long)
make -C port asic-smoke      # LibreLane sky130 leaf-block RTL-to-GDS
```

### SMT and FPU configuration

The core build is parameterized at compile time with `SMT` and `FPU`:

- `SMT=4` (default): four threads per core.
- `SMT=1`: single-thread build; enables the existing `FPGA_SYN_1THREAD`
  path. `SMT=2` is reserved for a future two-thread path.
- `FPU=1` (default): shared FPU enabled.
- `FPU=0`: defines `SPARC_NO_FPU`; the core keeps FP instructions
  disabled so they take `FP_DISABLED`, and the shared `fpu` block is not
  instantiated in the full chip.

Examples:

```sh
make -C port smoke SMT=1 FPU=0
make -C port fpga-flist SMT=1 FPU=0
make -C port fpga SMT=1 FPU=0
```

The defines are centralized in `design/sys/iop/include/sparc_config.h`.
`SPARC_1THREAD` maps to `FPGA_SYN_1THREAD` for compatibility with the
original Sun guards.

The FPGA targets look for `yosys`, `nextpnr-himbaechel`, and `gowin_pack`
under `/shared/oss-cad-suite/bin`; override `OSS_CAD_SUITE` to use another
installation. Version 0.70 or newer of Yosys is required for the Gowin
library models. The ASIC targets require `nix` and the LibreLane checkout at
`/opt/librelane`, plus a CIEL sky130 PDK under `~/.ciel` (override with
`PDK_ROOT`).

Generated files are written to `port/build/`. Remove them with:

```sh
make -C port clean
```

The top module used by the checks is `sparc`, the CPU core. Override it with
`make -C port TOP=sparc` or generate a list for another top.

### Direct commands

The Makefile is a thin wrapper around the following commands:

```sh
python3 port/scripts/gen_filelist.py \
    --mode rtl \
    --top sparc \
    -o port/build/core1_rtl.f \
    --config-v port/build/config.v \
    --yosys port/build/core1_rtl.ys

iverilog -g2005 -s sparc \
    -f port/build/core1_rtl.f \
    -o port/build/core1_rtl.vvp

verilator --lint-only --language 1364-2001 --no-timing \
    --top-module sparc -Wno-fatal \
    -f port/build/core1_rtl.f

yosys -q -s port/build/core1_rtl.ys
```

Verilator currently reports legacy width and implicit-net warnings. They do
not stop the check because `-Wno-fatal` is used. The target of Stage 1 is
parse and hierarchy compatibility, not a warning-free lint baseline.

`--no-timing` is required because the clock wrappers use `#10` delay controls.
Verilator lint is a structural check; it does not model those delays.

## Stage 2: core simulation smoke test

Stage 2 adds a self-contained, pure-Verilog testbench that boots one T1 core,
fills an I-cache line, executes a branch, and detects the resulting second
instruction fetch. It does not use Vera, PLI, SAS, or any prebuilt Solaris
binary.

### Boot mechanism

A T1 core does not start fetching by itself after reset. In the full chip the
I/O bridge sends a CPX `INT_RET` packet carrying a reset interrupt. The TLU
decodes the packet, loads the reset trap vector, and starts thread 0. The
testbench injects that packet once, 300 clock cycles after reset release.

Instruction fetches appear on the core PCX interface as `IMISS_RQ` packets.
The testbench acts as a minimal L2: for each miss it returns two 16-byte CPX
`IFILL_RET` packets, low half first and then high half, which together form
one 32-byte I-cache line. No memory model or page tables are needed: the reset
trap path maps its virtual addresses to fixed physical addresses, and the
responder keys on the physical address carried by the IMISS packet.

The served image is:

| Virtual address | Instruction | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `0xffff_f000_0040` | `0x10800010` | `ba 0xffff_f000_0080` |
| `0xffff_f000_0044` | `0x01000000` | `nop` (delay slot) |
| `0xffff_f000_0080` | `0x01000000` | `nop` |

The PASS condition is a subsequent `IMISS_RQ` at physical address
`0xfff0_0000_80`. A sequential fetch would continue at `0x48`, so a fetch at
`0x80` is only possible if the reset trap, the I-cache fill, and the branch
all executed correctly.

### Running the smoke test

```sh
make -C port smoke            # Icarus, a few seconds
make -C port smoke-verilator  # Verilator, several minutes on first build
make -C port regress          # both, Icarus first
```

The Icarus and Verilator targets both fail the `make` invocation unless the
log contains `SMOKE PASS`. Logs are written to `port/build/smoke_icarus.log`
and `port/build/smoke_verilator.log`. Typical output:

```text
PCX IMISS #0 t=1712500 tid=0 line=fff0000040
PCX IMISS #1 t=1807500 tid=0 line=fff0000040
PCX IMISS #2 t=1902500 tid=0 line=fff0000080
SMOKE PASS: branch to fff0000080 executed, second fetch issued
```

The reset line may be requested more than once while the thread is being
restarted; the responder answers every request. `JOBS=N` overrides the
Verilator build parallelism. Verilator is a 2-state simulator, so the SRAM
models' simulation-only `== 1'bx` checks misfire; the Verilator target defines
`INNO_MUXEX` to disable those diagnostics. It does not change functional RTL
behavior.

The testbench, responder, and PASS program are in
`port/tb/tb_core_smoke.v`. The single-core wrapper is
`design/sys/iop/rtl/iop_fpga.v`; it exposes only clock, reset, PCX, and CPX.

## Stage 3: FPGA and ASIC implementation

Stage 3 builds two implementation wrappers around the same core:

- `port/fpga/` targets the Sipeed Tang Mega 138K Pro Dock with the open
  Gowin flow (`yosys` + `nextpnr-himbaechel` + `gowin_pack`).
- `port/asic/` targets sky130 through LibreLane, invoked from the pinned nix
  shell in `/opt/librelane`.

Neither flow uses the original Synopsys/Synplicity scripts.

### FPGA: Tang Mega 138K Pro Dock

`port/fpga/t1_fpga_top.v` instantiates `iop_fpga`, injects the CPX reset
interrupt, answers IMISS requests with a hard-coded IFILL image, and reports
progress on the board LEDs. The boot image is the same one used by Stage 2:

| LED | Active-low indication |
| --- | --- |
| `led[0]` | ~0.7 s heartbeat |
| `led[1]` | boot interrupt sent |
| `led[2]` | first I-miss seen |
| `led[3]` | branch PASS reached |
| `led[4]` | timeout |
| `led[5]` | ~12 Hz activity |

Pins are in `port/fpga/tang_mega_138k.cst`: 50 MHz clock `P16`, button
`K16` as active-low reset, and LEDs `R26 J14 L20 M25 N21 N23`. The clock
and reset are the only inputs; there is no external memory.

The synthesis source list is Sun's `design/sys/iop/sparc/xst/sparc.flist`
plus `iop_fpga.v` and the board wrapper. `port/scripts/gen_fpga_ys.py`
generates the Yosys script with the FPGA defines from Sun's flow. The
nextpnr device is `GW5AST-LV138FPG676AC1/I0` with
`--vopt family=GW5AST-138C`; the packaged chipdb is named `138C` even though
the Amaranth board file says `138B`.

```sh
make -C port fpga-sim    # boots the wrapper in Icarus
make -C port fpga-smoke  # one-adder toolchain bitstream, seconds to build
make -C port fpga        # full core: synth, PnR, bitstream
```

`fpga-smoke` is a quick board/toolchain check built from
`port/fpga/t1_fpga_smoke.v`; it routes in seconds and passes 50 MHz with
246.43 MHz reported Fmax (69 LUT4, 63 DFF, 64 ALU). The full-core
synthesis after Gowin packing uses:

| Resource | Used | Available | Utilisation |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| LUT4 | 105,487 | 138,240 | 76% |
| DFF | 44,925 | 138,240 | 32% |
| MUX2_LUT5 | 16,637 | 69,120 | 24% |
| BSRAM | 44 | 340 | 12% |
| RAM16SDP4 | 1,568 | 17,280 | 9% |
| ALU | 1,070 | 103,680 | 1% |

Synthesis fits the device, but the packed design is too dense for
nextpnr's placement legaliser: both the analytic placer and the heap placer
(`--placer heap --no-tmdriv --placer-heap-beta 1.0 --placer-heap-no-ctrl-set`)
stop with `Unable to find legal placement for all cells, design is probably at
utilisation limit.` at 76% LUT4 use. No full-core bitstream is produced in
this environment. `make -C port fpga` therefore remains a synthesis and PnR
attempt, not a guaranteed bitstream; `fpga-smoke` is the supported bitstream
path. Should a full-core attempt succeed, the bitstream is written to
`port/build/fpga/t1_fpga.fs`; program it with:

```sh
openFPGALoader -b tangmega138k port/build/fpga/t1_fpga.fs
```

The simulation path (`fpga-sim`) prints `FPGA WRAPPER PASS` when the branch
at `0xffff_f000_0080` executes and issues the second instruction fetch. The
bitstreams have not been tested on physical hardware in this environment.

### ASIC: LibreLane and sky130

`port/asic/run.sh` is a thin wrapper around:

```sh
nix develop /opt/librelane -c librelane --manual-pdk --pdk-root ~/.ciel -p sky130A ...
```

LibreLane 3.0.17 expects `LIB`/`DEFAULT_CORNER` PDK metadata, while the CIEL
sky130 metadata installed here provides `CELL_LIBS`. The wrapper generates a
temporary override mapping the `tt`, `ss` and `ff` liberty files and sets
`DEFAULT_CORNER` to `nom_tt_025C_1v80`. It also uses `--manual-pdk` to avoid
LibreLane's automatic PDK fetch, which fails when only a SOCKS proxy is
available.

The ASIC smoke design is a registered 64-bit T1 adder
(`t1_asic_smoke.v`, using `sparc_exu_aluadder64.v`). It is a leaf block, not
the CPU: it exercises the complete LibreLane RTL-to-GDS path without
requiring SRAM macros or a full-chip pinout.

```sh
make -C port asic-smoke
```

The generated GDS is
`port/asic/runs/smoke/final/gds/t1_asic_smoke.gds` (ignored by git). The
15 ns configuration closes setup and hold timing in all documented corners:
the saturated `nom_ss_100C_1v60` corner has 1.47 ns setup slack and the
`nom_tt_025C_1v80` corner has 7.95 ns. The die is 22,336.5 µm² with 2,274
instances. A few pins slightly exceed the 0.75 ns max-slew limit, which is
reported as a warning. The flow also writes a metrics file at
`port/asic/runs/smoke/final/metrics.json`.

`make -C port asic-config asic-synth` generates a full-core LibreLane
configuration (147 sources, top `iop_fpga`, clock `gclk`) and runs through
`Yosys.Synthesis`. The full T1 core is far larger than the leaf block: with
no ASIC SRAM macros, generic synthesis expands the register files and cache
arrays into about 350,820 flip-flops and 1,151,492 cells. DFF mapping
completes, but ABC technology mapping does not finish in a reasonable time
in this environment, and the resulting netlist is not P&R-ready. The
full-core target is therefore a synthesis/feasibility check; physical
implementation needs SRAM macro replacement and a partitioned floorplan.

## File-list generation

The checked-in configuration files are the single source of truth:

- `port/config/core1.config`
- the `.flist` files it references under `design/` and `verif/`

`port/scripts/gen_filelist.py` parses the Sun `sims` format, resolves
`$DV_ROOT` and paths relative to each `.flist`, and emits:

- `port/build/core1_rtl.f`: an Icarus/Verilator file list with
  `+define+`, `+incdir+`, `-v`, and source entries;
- `port/build/config.v`: the same configuration macros as Verilog
  `` `define `` directives;
- `port/build/core1_rtl.ys`: a Yosys script that reads the RTL, treats the
  memory models as libraries, and runs `hierarchy -check`.

`--mode rtl` selects design and library sources only. It produces 631 source
files, 89 library files, and 7 include directories for the `core1` RTL.
`--mode full` additionally includes the verification environment. That list is
available for inspection, but it is not yet buildable: the legacy testbench
still depends on Vera and PLI, and the Solaris `sims` binaries are no longer
in the tree.

Do not edit the generated lists by hand. Change the original `.flist` or
`core1.config` and regenerate.

The Yosys script omits the pad models under `design/sys/iop/pads/`. Those
models use drive-strength syntax that Yosys does not parse, and the pads are
not part of the `sparc` core netlist. The Icarus and Verilator lists include
the pads.

## Stage 1 RTL changes

Stage 1 changes are portability fixes. They do not intentionally alter the
behavior of the design.

### Verilog-2001 keyword collisions

SystemVerilog reserves `logic`, which is also a legal Verilog-2001 identifier.
Two uses were renamed rather than enabling SystemVerilog mode, so the project
continues to validate the RTL as strict Verilog-2001:

- `design/sys/iop/sparc/ffu/rtl/sparc_ffu_ctl_visctl.v`: the `logic` wire
  and its five uses are now `vis_logic`.
- `design/sys/iop/sparc/exu/rtl/sparc_exu_alu.v`: instance `logic` is now
  `alu_logic`.

### Portable diagnostics

Verilator in 1364-2001 mode does not support the `$error` system task. All 90
active `$error` calls in 28 files were replaced with `$display` calls. The
first two string arguments were merged so the messages retain their tag and
text, for example:

```verilog
// before
$error("L2_TAG_ERR", " way select error %h ", way[11:0]);

// after
$display("ERROR: L2_TAG_ERR:  way select error %h ", way[11:0]);
```

Every converted message is prefixed with `ERROR:`, so diagnostics remain easy
to search. Note that `$display` does not abort a simulation; a future stage
should move these checks into assertions.

### Yosys-compatible parameter overrides

Yosys rejects the positional instance parameter syntax `#N` and requires the
parenthesized form. All 93 affected instances in 9 files were converted from
`#N` to `#(N)`, for example:

```verilog
dff_s #4 park_reg(...);    // becomes
dff_s #(4) park_reg(...);
```

The two forms are equivalent in Verilog-2001.

### Control characters

Form-feed characters were removed from 11 JBI source files. The files remain
functionally identical; the bytes caused Yosys to report an invalid token.

## Stage 3 RTL changes

These changes preserve behavior but make the legacy RTL acceptable to the
implementation tools.

### Implicit nets

Twenty files rely on Verilog-2001 implicit nets. Icarus, Verilator, and
Yosys's default mode accept them, but LibreLane reads sources with
`read_verilog -noautowire`, which turns undeclared identifiers into hard
errors. Each affected file now starts with:

```verilog
// Legacy RTL relies on Verilog-2001 implicit nets; LibreLane reads with
// -noautowire, so restore the standard default explicitly.
`default_nettype wire
```

The directive restores the language default for that file and does not
change the synthesized logic. The files are in `sparc/lsu/`, `sparc/spu/`,
`sparc/tlu/`, and `sparc/exu/`; they contained 302 implicit nets in total.

### Asynchronous-reset cells

The seven async-reset flops in
`design/sys/iop/common/rtl/swrvr_clib.v` used a ternary expression as the
reset value with an asynchronous reset in the sensitivity list. Older Yosys
releases rejected this with "Multiple edge sensitive events found for this
signal". The cells now use an equivalent `if (reset) ... else ...` form.
The Stage 2 Icarus and Verilator regression passes with this change.

## Legacy binaries and boot firmware

The tree no longer contains prebuilt executables or boot firmware. Earlier
revisions bundled Sun's `tools/` (238 32-bit SPARC/Solaris and i386 ELF
programs), the Xilinx ML505/ML410 EDK artifacts, and eight 1 MiB PROM images
under the original release's `design/sys/edk/os/proms/`. All of these have
been removed. The open flows read only `port/config/core1.config` and the `.flist` files under
`design/` and `verif/`, so `design/`, `lib/`, and `port/` are the build
inputs. The open port does not use the PROM images; its boot image is a few
hard-coded instructions.

The removed PROM images can be obtained again from the original OpenSPARC T1
archive; QEMU's SPARC64 documentation links the archive and the `S10image/`
firmware directory:
<https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/system/target-sparc64.html>. For
source-based firmware work:

- `openbios/openbios` is a GPL-2.0 Open Firmware implementation, but it has
  no sun4v/T1 target. Its SPARC64 port is the QEMU sun4u (UltraSPARC IIi)
  firmware and cannot run the T1 hypervisor.
- `openbios/openboot` and the `masa-murayama/qemu-sun4v-hypervisor` /
  `masa-murayama/qemu-sun4v-openboot` trees contain Sun OBP and hypervisor
  sources with T1 (`niagara`, `t1_fpga`) targets. The hypervisor tree's
  `t1_fpga/q.bin` is byte-identical to the hypervisor payload at offset
  `0x2_0000` in the removed PROM images. These trees still require a
  Solaris/SPARC build environment (Sun Studio, Solaris assembler/linker,
  SCCS) and the QEMU variants have no build documentation, so none is a
  drop-in replacement here.

The Xilinx EDK/Aurora reference-design trees (`design/sys/edk/` and
`design/sys/edk_bee3/`) have been removed from the open tree: they depend on
MicroBlaze, MIG, and Aurora IP that the open flow cannot build, and the
`edk_bee3` copy duplicated the Aurora sources. The only firmware artifact the
open flow still uses is the GPL `mbfw_diag_memimage.c` memory image, kept as
`port/firmware/mbfw_diag_memimage.c` for the `make memimage` target.

## Known limitations

- The original Vera/PLI regression suite is not ported. The Stage 2 smoke test
  is a new, small test; it does not replace the full T1 verification
  environment. The legacy `sims` flow and its Solaris binaries are not
  included.
- The smoke test covers one core, one thread, and only the PCX/CPX interface.
  It uses a hard-coded instruction image and a simple IFILL responder. It has
  no L2 cache model, DRAM model, MMU page tables, I/O bridge, general
  interrupt support, console, or boot loader.
- `--mode full` file lists include verification sources, but they have not
  been made buildable in an open-source simulator.
- Verilator still reports legacy width, implicit-net, and unused-signal
  warnings. They are reported but not yet cleaned up. The simulation target
  uses `--timing` to model the `#10` clock delay; first-build time is several
  minutes.
- The Yosys `sparc` check is parse and hierarchy validation. The Stage 3
  flows perform real synthesis, but the full-core ASIC result is not
  P&R-ready: sky130 SRAM macros are not available, so memories become
  flip-flops, and the core is not partitioned for physical design.
- The full T1 core packs to 76% of the GW5AST-138 LUT4 sites, and nextpnr
  legalization rejects it at that density in this environment. `make -C port
  fpga` is a full-core synthesis and place-and-route attempt, not a guaranteed
  bitstream; `make -C port fpga-smoke` is the quick supported bitstream path.
  None of the bitstreams have been tested on physical hardware.
- There is no external memory, L2 cache, I/O bridge, console, boot loader, or
  general interrupt support in the FPGA wrapper. It boots a hard-coded
  instruction image and proves that the core, PCX/CPX interface, I-cache
  fill, and branch execution work.
- No ASIC clock generation, I/O pads, DFT/scan insertion, or full-chip
  floorplan is provided. The LibreLane flow covers a leaf block and
  full-core synthesis feasibility only.
- The original prebuilt Solaris binaries, EDK payloads, and PROM images are
  not included. Rebuilding the boot firmware requires the upstream
  OpenSPARC/Sun OBP sources and a Solaris/SPARC toolchain.

## Repository layout

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `design/` | T1 RTL, SRAM models, clock/pad wrappers |
| `lib/` | Library models, including the `u1` behavioral library |
| `verif/` | Original verification environment, monitors, and testbenches |
| `doc/` | Original design and verification PDFs |
| `port/` | File-list generator, Makefile, simulation tests, build outputs |
| `port/config/` | `core1.config`, the Sun `sims` manifest used by the open flow |
| `port/fpga/` | Tang Mega 138K FPGA wrappers and pin constraints |
| `port/asic/` | LibreLane configuration, smoke block, and nix wrapper |
| `port/firmware/` | GPL memory image used by the `memimage` target |
| `port/stim/` | Playback stimulus archive (`thread1_mini`) and `l2_buf.mem` |
| `port/PLAYBACK.md` | Playback lane usage and status |
| `port/scripts/` | File-list, Yosys, and LibreLane config generators |

## Original Sun environments and documentation

The original database defines three `sims` environments:

- `thread1`: one SPARC core without the Stream Processing Unit, plus cache,
  memory, and crossbar; no I/O subsystem.
- `core1`: one full SPARC core, plus cache, memory, and crossbar; no I/O
  subsystem.
- `chip8`: the full T1 chip with eight cores, caches, memory, crossbar, and
  the I/O subsystem.

The open port uses the `core1` environment for its file lists and smoke test.
The original `sims` regression groups (`thread1_mini`, `thread1_full`,
`core1_mini`, `core1_full`, `chip8_mini`, `chip8_full`) required VCS or
NC-Verilog, the SAS and Midas tools, Vera, and a Solaris host. That flow is
not included or supported here.

The original documentation is under `doc/`:

- `OpenSPARCT1_DVGuide.pdf`: processor design and verification user's guide
- `OpenSPARCT1_ExternalInterface.pdf`: external interface specification
- `OpenSPARCT1_DataSheet.pdf`: processor datasheet
- `OpenSPARCT1_MegaCell.pdf`: megacell specification
- `OpenSPARCT1_Micro_Arch.pdf`: micro-architecture specification

## Roadmap

- Stage 2 (complete): pure-Verilog core smoke test, Icarus and Verilator
  simulation targets, a regression entry point, and no Vera/PLI dependency in
  the new flow.
- Stage 3 (complete): FPGA wrapper with nextpnr/Gowin synthesis and
  bitstream flow, a fast toolchain bitstream, an ASIC leaf-block flow
  through LibreLane/sky130, and a full-core ASIC synthesis configuration.
- Stage 4: close timing on the full FPGA core or a reduced/congestion-aware
  variant, replace inferred memories with ASIC SRAM macros, partition the
  ASIC design, and add external memory/interrupt/console support.

